From: genshanxinli <127909456+genshanxinli@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Nov 2026 16:20:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] net/airoha: make the PPPoE decapsulation encoding selectable

A PPPoE ingress rule reaches this driver with nothing that says how the
session header is to be stripped.  The tuple names the level -- that is
what flow_cls_offload::pppoe_decap carries, added in
9905-net-airoha-report-pppoe-over-vlan-capability.patch, because the flow
action enum has FLOW_ACTION_VLAN_POP but no FLOW_ACTION_PPPOE_POP -- but
there is no action to take a session id from and no field that says which
IB1 bit makes the parser peel the header.  Bit22 (BIND_PPPOE) is the psn
bind the push direction sets, bit23 (BIND_TUNNEL_DECAP, the vendor's
"rmt") is the remove-the-outer-header bit DS-Lite and 6rd set, and no
PPPoE flow in this tree has ever set either of them on ingress.

Rather than pick one and call it a fix, this patch makes the encoding a
documented arm and makes the result readable:

  0  leave the entry exactly as it is today (default)
  1  psn + etype + rmt        (bit22 | bit23)
  2  etype + rmt              (bit23)
  3  psn + etype, rmt clear   (bit22)  <- the arm issue 81 asks for

Arm 3 is the discriminator, not a default: 0 keeps every byte of the old
behaviour, so nothing changes for a flow table that never writes the knob.
Arms 1 and 2 exist so arm 3 can be told apart from "rmt is the bit that
strips" and from "psn takes part at all"; that is a 2-bit matrix over the
two candidate bits, not a menu of tunables.

The knob is /sys/kernel/debug/ppe/pppoe_decap (0644, "0".."3", refused
outside that range).  /sys/kernel/debug/ppe/pppoe (0444) lists every entry
that is a PPPoE decap candidate -- psn set, etype ETH_P_PPP_SES, or rmt set
on a non-tunnel packet type -- with the arm in force and the three fields
the question turns on: bit22, bit23 and the bound session id.  The arms are
read once per entry, so a write affects the entries built afterwards and
leaves the table alone; the readback is what tells the two apart, and it is
also what scripts/pppoe-decap-hw-bench.sh samples for its 0/1/2 arms.

Subflows already inherit the bound L2 parent's IB1 bits via
e->data.ib1 & ~mask in airoha_ppe_foe_commit_subflow_entry() (where hwe_p
is an unbound hardware entry whose bits 23:8 hold UNBIND_PACKETS rather
than bind flags), so the subflow commit path is left untouched and never
reads packet-count bits as PPPoE or tunnel decap flags.


Limitations, deliberate:

  - No session id is bound for the decap direction.  There is no
    FLOW_ACTION_PPPOE_POP to carry one and the tuple only records that the
    level exists, so pppoe_id stays 0 in every arm; the arm binds "a PPPoE
    session header", not "session 0x1234".  A non-zero pppoe_id in the
    readback therefore means the entry was built for the push direction.
  - The etype word is shared with the egress encoding (WDMA band, DSA port
    mask).  When the egress device owns it, the arm sets the IB1 bits and
    leaves the etype alone, so no other flow type changes shape; the wired
    decap path measured by the bench is unaffected because it takes the
    plain branch.
  - DS-Lite and 6rd entries are excluded from the arms outright.
  - VLAN_LAYER/VPM are untouched: a session stacked on an 802.1Q tag still
    gets no strip, which is issue 83's problem and 9905's warning.

MTU, LRO and every other flow type are untouched.  Not exercised on
hardware: the XR1710G on the bench has no PPPoE WAN (wan is proto=dhcp,
/proc/net/pppoe has no session, no peer), so this is compile-tested and
code-reviewed only, and the arms are meant to be measured one at a time on
a bed that has a session.  Filed as 9907 rather than the 9904 the issue
names: the patch consumes flow_cls_offload::pppoe_decap, so it has to apply
after 9905, and 9904 would sort before it.

Signed-off-by: genshanxinli <127909456+genshanxinli@users.noreply.github.com>
